# Regression 3: Quantile Regression

Least squares describes the conditional mean of a response. Quantile regression describes a conditional *quantile*: the line below which a given fraction τ of the responses lie. The median line (τ = 0.5) is not dragged by outliers, and a set of quantile lines shows how the whole distribution of the response changes with the predictors, including its spread. After this chapter you can fit a quantile line, compare several quantiles, choose the covariance estimator, control the optimiser, and read the report.

Every program is complete, and its output is the output of that program.

## The calls

```cpp
reg::QuantileFitResult reg::quantile_fit(const Series &y, const Series &x, const reg::QuantileOptions &options = {}, Config cfg = {});
Series                 reg::quantile_predict(const reg::QuantileFitResult &fit, const Series &x_new, Config cfg = {});
std::string            reg::quantile_summary(const reg::QuantileFitResult &fit);
std::ostream          &reg::print_quantile_summary(std::ostream &os, const reg::QuantileFitResult &fit);
```

Inputs are read as for least squares ([Shapes and inputs](regression_01_least_squares/shapes_and_inputs.md)). The loss minimised is the check function, which weighs a residual above the line by τ and below it by 1 − τ.

| `QuantileOptions` | Meaning | Default |
|---|---|---|
| `tau` | the quantile, strictly between 0 and 1 | 0.5 |
| `covariance` | `Robust` (a sandwich estimator) or `Iid` | `Robust` |
| `alpha` | the intervals have coverage `1 − alpha` | 0.05 |
| `add_intercept`, `drop_missing`, `rcond` | as for least squares | true, true, 1e-12 |
| `weights` | case weights; row *i* counts as if repeated `weights[i]` times | none |
| `max_iter`, `tol`, `eps` | the optimiser: iteration cap, convergence tolerance, and floor on the absolute residual in the weights | 500, 1e-7, 1e-6 |
| `response_name`, `predictor_names` | labels used by the summary | `"y"`, none |

| `QuantileFitResult` | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `coefficients`, `std_errors`, `t_values`, `p_values` | one per parameter; the p-values and intervals use Student's *t* on `df_resid` degrees of freedom |
| `conf_int`, `covariance` | `[k, 2]` limits and the `[k, k]` covariance matrix |
| `fitted_values`, `residuals` | `[n]`, for the rows used |
| `tau`, `objective`, `objective_null`, `pseudo_rsquared` | the quantile, the check-function loss at the fit and at a constant, and `1 − objective / objective_null` |
| `residual_density0` | the estimated density of the residuals at zero, on which the standard errors rest |
| `iterations`, `converged` | what the optimiser did |
| `nobs`, `rank`, `k_params`, `num_predictors`, `df_model`, `df_resid`, `dropped_observations`, `warnings` | counts and conditioning |
| `has_intercept`, `coefficient_names`, `response_name`, `alpha` | what the fit was made with |

